# Restaurant Management System — Project Specification

This document is the single source of truth for class names, attributes, method
names, and shared code that everyone must follow. Since the team is not using
header files or GitHub, this document replaces that role: it's what keeps five
separately-written `.cpp` files compatible when they get combined into one project.


Each person writes their part as **one `.cpp` file** — no `.h` files needed.

---

## 1. Naming Rules (apply to everyone, no exceptions)

- Class names: `PascalCase` — e.g. `Customer`, `OrderManager`
- Method names: `camelCase` — e.g. `addCustomer`, `calculateTotal`
- Variable names: `camelCase` — e.g. `customerID`, `phoneNumber`
- Every ID field is a plain `int`, always named `<entity>ID` — e.g. `customerID`, `orderID`, `tableID`, `employeeID`
- Getter methods: `getX()` — e.g. `getName()`
- Setter methods: `setX(...)` — e.g. `setName(string n)`
- Yes/no check methods: `isX()` — e.g. `isAvailable()`

---

## 2. Shared Code — Copy This Exactly Into the Top of Your File

Every member pastes this exact block at the top of their `.cpp` file (below their
`#include`s). Nobody edits this block — if something seems missing, tell Kareem so
he can add it here for everyone at once.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <string>
using namespace std;

enum class OrderType { DineIn, Takeaway, Delivery };
enum class OrderStatus { New, Preparing, Ready, Served, Completed, Cancelled };
enum class TableStatus { Available, Reserved, Occupied, OutOfService };
enum class ReservationStatus { Pending, Confirmed, Completed, Cancelled };
enum class PaymentMethod { Cash, Card, MobilePayment };
enum class PaymentStatus { Completed, Refunded };
enum class DeliveryStatus { Assigned, OnTheWay, Delivered, Cancelled };
enum class EmployeeRole { Manager, Chef, Waiter, Cashier, DeliveryDriver };
enum class MenuCategory { Appetizer, MainCourse, Dessert, Drink };
```

---

## 3. The One Rule That Matters Most

**Never store another module's object directly — always store its ID instead.**

Example: `Order` stores `int customerID` and `int tableID`, never `Customer` or `Table`
objects. This is what lets everyone write and test their part completely on their
own, and it's what Kareem relies on when combining the five files at the end.

